1
00:00:24,725 --> 00:00:33,767
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is a sutta called the simile of the poisonous snakes, or vipers, as it's said.

2
00:00:33,807 --> 00:00:38,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, suppose there were four vipers of fierce heat and deadly venom.

3
00:00:39,288 --> 00:00:46,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, a man would come along, wanting to live, not wanting to die, desiring happiness and averse to suffering.

4
00:00:47,509 --> 00:00:52,810
[SPEAKER_00]: They would tell him, good man, these four vipers are of fierce heat and deadly venom.

5
00:00:53,750 --> 00:00:53,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Hmm.

6
00:01:17,409 --> 00:01:24,719
[SPEAKER_00]: A little bit later on in the discourse says, I have made up this simile because in order to convey a meaning, this is the meaning here.

7
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:34,492
[SPEAKER_00]: The four vipers of fierce heat and deadly venom, this is the designation for the four great elements, earth, water, heat and air.

8
00:01:37,969 --> 00:01:47,274
[SPEAKER_00]: The saying is, you know, it's not saying those are like beautiful energies that you must connect with, and appreciate, and cultivate those energies, so that you can...

9
00:01:48,055 --> 00:01:49,356
[SPEAKER_00]: He's saying these are poisonous.

10
00:01:49,816 --> 00:01:52,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Deadly vipers that, like, kill you with the wind.

11
00:01:52,557 --> 00:01:53,098
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

12
00:01:53,998 --> 00:01:55,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can look... That's the thing with snakes.

13
00:01:56,039 --> 00:01:57,961
[SPEAKER_01]: You can... That's why the world gave that simile.

14
00:01:58,661 --> 00:02:05,325
[SPEAKER_01]: You can look after, you can bathe, you can feed, and they can still bite you, because they have no concept of what you're doing.

15
00:02:06,482 --> 00:02:09,403
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can do everything right, in other words, and still get bitten.

16
00:02:11,263 --> 00:02:17,305
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, you know, a dog will bite you when he's upset, or a leopard will attack you if he's threatened.

17
00:02:18,265 --> 00:02:23,527
[SPEAKER_01]: The snake will just bite you and misperceives you, or whatever, even if you're feeding it and bathing it and looking after it.

18
00:02:24,767 --> 00:02:27,788
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's a similar, like, oh, the man would still, like, be scared.

19
00:02:28,488 --> 00:02:32,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, these these vipers need to be lifted up, cleaned, fed.

20
00:02:35,163 --> 00:02:41,825
[SPEAKER_01]: They might bite you, so you'll be scared, because there is no guarantee that you will not get bitten, even if you do everything right.

21
00:02:43,145 --> 00:02:45,066
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all your exercise.

22
00:02:45,106 --> 00:02:48,066
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it's not in your control.

23
00:02:48,387 --> 00:02:51,387
[SPEAKER_01]: You can do everything right and still die on account of it.

24
00:02:53,048 --> 00:02:54,628
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is the fourth great element?

25
00:02:55,549 --> 00:02:57,849
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the the closest

26
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:04,305
[SPEAKER_00]: The thing to you, that is made out of four great elements?

27
00:03:04,325 --> 00:03:04,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Your body.

28
00:03:04,946 --> 00:03:05,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

29
00:03:05,407 --> 00:03:05,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Entirely.

30
00:03:06,008 --> 00:03:06,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Entirely.

31
00:03:06,849 --> 00:03:09,894
[SPEAKER_01]: Not parts of your body, and then there are other parts of body that are yours.

32
00:03:10,454 --> 00:03:13,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Your body, in its entirety, are the four vipers.

33
00:03:15,005 --> 00:03:20,707
[SPEAKER_01]: The four whimsical elements that you don't really exist for, so to speak, that's the thing.

34
00:03:20,727 --> 00:03:25,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, snake doesn't have awareness of you as its carer, feeder.

35
00:03:25,568 --> 00:03:30,529
[SPEAKER_01]: It's literally just... it sees what's in the front, it reacts, it acts, it changes.

36
00:03:30,609 --> 00:03:34,831
[SPEAKER_01]: And you, you cannot connect to it, like, form a relationship with it.

37
00:03:36,631 --> 00:03:40,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, even people who actually do have snakes as pets, they look after them.

38
00:03:41,853 --> 00:03:43,253
[SPEAKER_01]: One wrong move and you can get bitten.

39
00:03:44,667 --> 00:03:47,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not, there's no, no perception of it in the snake.

40
00:03:47,549 --> 00:04:01,918
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's no perception in the four great elements of you, that, that, oh, he is a master, or he is our, basically, our friend, our individual we are bound with.

41
00:04:02,338 --> 00:04:02,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Not at all.

42
00:04:02,838 --> 00:04:13,565
[SPEAKER_01]: They have their own course, and you are paired with a body that is comprised of four great elements, for as long as that body is there, basically, doing its own thing.

43
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:17,662
[SPEAKER_01]: When that changes, you will not be able to remain.

44
00:04:19,843 --> 00:04:22,345
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter how friendly you are to it.

45
00:04:22,385 --> 00:04:24,186
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter what, how much you look after it.

46
00:04:24,286 --> 00:04:25,747
[SPEAKER_00]: The extent of your ownership.

47
00:04:26,407 --> 00:04:27,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

48
00:04:27,388 --> 00:04:28,909
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you can't prevent it.

49
00:04:28,929 --> 00:04:29,930
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't prevent it.

50
00:04:31,677 --> 00:04:37,562
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's... it seems to be that's the perception you should be developing towards those...

51
00:04:37,582 --> 00:04:37,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

52
00:04:38,142 --> 00:04:48,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you have to, because unless... for as long as you're seeing your own body, the elements that are comprising it in friendly terms, you're not going to be seeing the danger.

53
00:04:49,531 --> 00:04:54,595
[SPEAKER_01]: By not seeing the danger, you will remain infatuated and assuming the ownership of it.

54
00:04:54,755 --> 00:04:57,477
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just the result of not seeing the danger.

55
00:04:58,795 --> 00:05:14,443
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you start regarding it as deadly vipers, whereby there is no wrong move that you can do, and they'll still kill you, they still might kill you, at any given time, and being completely unaware that they're killing you, they'll just do it.

56
00:05:15,203 --> 00:05:17,925
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, even the concept of you dying means nothing to them.

57
00:05:19,254 --> 00:05:30,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you would, you would be, what, you would think twice before you regard that as friendly and mine and worth finding joy in and using it for pursuit of pleasure.

58
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:32,621
[SPEAKER_00]: Contemplate those elements.

59
00:05:34,722 --> 00:05:37,083
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, what are you supposed to be looking for?

60
00:05:37,103 --> 00:05:46,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, is it... Well, what you shouldn't, yeah, what you shouldn't be doing is starting with your idea of elements and then applying it to you, because it's already going to be misconceived.

61
00:05:47,767 --> 00:06:00,652
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to... you want to do it... you want to find or discern the elements, within, first, discerning the proper phenomenon of your body, for what it is.

62
00:06:00,672 --> 00:06:15,098
[SPEAKER_01]: So, recognizing certain facticity of the body, and then, within that, discerning these characteristics of solid, liquid, fiery, just again, as properties of the body, because of which body is there.

63
00:06:16,282 --> 00:06:17,523
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not optional.

64
00:06:18,403 --> 00:06:19,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I can sit like this or I can... No.

65
00:06:20,044 --> 00:06:28,930
[SPEAKER_01]: These four things are to be cultivated because by discerning these four properties of this body, you get to discern that the universal properties of anything that appears.

66
00:06:29,471 --> 00:06:33,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything that appears shares the property of solid, liquid, airy or fiery.

67
00:06:34,634 --> 00:06:43,440
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you would then realize that this tiny body, less than two meters tall, in comparison, you know, to the mountains and seas and oceans,

68
00:06:44,439 --> 00:06:46,020
[SPEAKER_01]: It's made of the same elements, literally.

69
00:06:46,060 --> 00:06:55,826
[SPEAKER_01]: And you could see, like, how much wind blows, and rains, and earthquakes, and landslides, and trees falling, and mountains crumbling.

70
00:06:55,866 --> 00:07:13,137
[SPEAKER_01]: What is there to expect of this tiny, quite precious, precarious, little, kind of, compound of flesh, which is made of exactly the same elements, like the world around you that you see constantly changing, and crumbling, and disintegrating, and forming, and reforming.

71
00:07:14,433 --> 00:07:28,717
[SPEAKER_01]: So, contemplating, seeing these characteristics of the great universal nature of form deforming, that is its nature, but you can see that on the level of idea, of visualization, and that's not it.

72
00:07:29,357 --> 00:07:34,898
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to see through the body that is yours, to begin with.

73
00:07:34,918 --> 00:07:37,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's how then that seeing it in the right order,

74
00:07:38,598 --> 00:07:41,481
[SPEAKER_01]: ...will basically undermine your sense of ownership of that body.

75
00:07:41,661 --> 00:07:45,665
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're just seeing it in the level of theory, it's... you're not undermining anything.

76
00:07:45,685 --> 00:07:49,068
[SPEAKER_01]: You... again, you're just sort of contemplating, fantasizing, theoretically.

77
00:07:50,526 --> 00:08:12,784
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you first, okay, this body here, seated position, breathing, having degree of warmth, degree of solidity, cohesion, whatever you want to describe these characteristics, and then allow those characteristics to endure, because even that in itself, you realize, oh, that body is given to you the way, the way you're seeing it.

78
00:08:13,225 --> 00:08:16,928
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you, you, you cannot kind of go behind it.

79
00:08:17,732 --> 00:08:20,274
[SPEAKER_01]: And have a say in its creation or its persistence.

80
00:08:21,074 --> 00:08:27,118
[SPEAKER_01]: It's there, but it can also not be there if it decides to die or something happens to it.

81
00:08:27,138 --> 00:08:35,002
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no amount of your control, intention, volition can step outside and go and kind of repossess that body externally and truly become the owner-master of it.

82
00:08:35,763 --> 00:08:36,863
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always secondary to it.

83
00:08:37,424 --> 00:08:39,285
[SPEAKER_01]: If you pursue that same contemplation,

84
00:08:40,263 --> 00:08:51,416
[SPEAKER_01]: That kind of impenetrable nature of the body, on account of which your whole life depends, then you already are discerning the characteristics of the four elements, which are pretty much impenetrable.

85
00:08:52,638 --> 00:08:53,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you cannot...

86
00:08:53,939 --> 00:08:55,802
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, can you, literally, can you even conceive

87
00:08:57,279 --> 00:09:00,301
[SPEAKER_01]: In fact, it's stopping the wind, stopping the air from moving.

88
00:09:00,501 --> 00:09:01,882
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, is that... You can't.

89
00:09:01,942 --> 00:09:02,923
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't even imagine that.

90
00:09:02,983 --> 00:09:03,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it would be ridiculous.

91
00:09:04,984 --> 00:09:07,005
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, that's exactly what your body is made of.

92
00:09:07,105 --> 00:09:11,808
[SPEAKER_01]: The same characteristics of the same wind, of the same heat.

93
00:09:11,828 --> 00:09:12,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Or getting angry.

94
00:09:13,949 --> 00:09:14,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah.

95
00:09:14,650 --> 00:09:21,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Getting angry, getting lustful, all of that requires that basis of assumed ownership first.

96
00:09:21,854 --> 00:09:25,497
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can use that as a symptom, as an indication.

97
00:09:26,590 --> 00:09:27,351
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I getting angry?

98
00:09:27,371 --> 00:09:28,271
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I getting lustful?

99
00:09:28,372 --> 00:09:39,360
[SPEAKER_01]: If so, it means I'm not really contemplating the body on the right terms, because if I were to do so, I would not be prone to lust or aversion.

100
00:09:39,580 --> 00:09:43,083
[SPEAKER_01]: You just wouldn't be able to give in to anger or attraction.

101
00:09:44,104 --> 00:09:46,546
[SPEAKER_00]: To something that's going to kill you, that can kill you.

102
00:09:46,566 --> 00:09:52,010
[SPEAKER_01]: To that which is kind of completely outside of your scope, even of imaginary control.

103
00:09:52,050 --> 00:09:55,433
[SPEAKER_01]: If you look at it closely, the clearer it becomes, you realize you can't even fathom

104
00:09:56,133 --> 00:09:56,733
[SPEAKER_01]: How it would be.

105
00:09:57,073 --> 00:10:07,476
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason you assume the ownership and the sense of control over the body is because you never really, ever, think about the nature of, oh, this is inaccessible to me.

106
00:10:10,017 --> 00:10:14,999
[SPEAKER_01]: But the more you think about it, the less you'll be able to then drift away in those assumptions of ownership.

107
00:10:15,659 --> 00:10:16,439
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's unpleasant.

108
00:10:17,559 --> 00:10:22,241
[SPEAKER_01]: From the point of view of ownership, the sense of non-ownership is fearful and frightening.

109
00:10:22,261 --> 00:10:22,761
[SPEAKER_01]: It's scary.

110
00:10:25,411 --> 00:10:32,718
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, like, again, people would need to have had some sense restraint and sīla developed beforehand.

111
00:10:32,838 --> 00:10:36,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you're just going to be filling yourself with anxiety and you will not know how to deal with it.

112
00:10:39,164 --> 00:10:44,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you haven't... you haven't developed strength on account of restraining your behavior beforehand.

113
00:10:55,746 --> 00:10:58,108
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you have to regard it as deadly vipers.

114
00:10:59,329 --> 00:11:09,479
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, everything, like, shorter than that will still, could still have lingering attitudes of friendliness and ownership towards it.

115
00:11:10,640 --> 00:11:16,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, okay, it's not, you know, you know, if I do the right things, you know, I'm in control of these vipers.

116
00:11:17,046 --> 00:11:18,808
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you're never in control of the vipers.

117
00:11:19,523 --> 00:11:22,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Balancing the energies... You're never in good... No, no.

118
00:11:22,685 --> 00:11:25,868
[SPEAKER_01]: You're literally just balancing when you're going to get bitten.

119
00:11:29,210 --> 00:11:34,615
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's that I couldn't... You have to... That's why... Good man, do what you think it's the right thing to do.

120
00:11:34,835 --> 00:11:37,937
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, the right thing is to leave these vipers and run for my life.

121
00:11:39,639 --> 00:11:39,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

122
00:11:46,485 --> 00:11:47,745
[SPEAKER_00]: That simile continues.

123
00:11:49,086 --> 00:11:52,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Then Bhikkhu is afraid of the four vipers of fierce heat and deadly venom.

124
00:11:53,267 --> 00:11:55,468
[SPEAKER_00]: That man would flee in one direction or another.

125
00:11:57,969 --> 00:12:02,671
[SPEAKER_00]: They would tell him, good man, five murderous enemies are pursuing you.

126
00:12:02,711 --> 00:12:06,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking, wherever we see him, we'll take his life right on the spot.

127
00:12:07,813 --> 00:12:09,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Do whatever has to be done, good man.

128
00:12:12,032 --> 00:12:14,994
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

129
00:12:17,095 --> 00:12:18,796
[SPEAKER_00]: There are five murderous enemies.

130
00:12:19,137 --> 00:12:28,663
[SPEAKER_00]: This is a designation for the five aggregates subject to assumption, form, feeling, perception, determinations, consciousness.

131
00:12:29,803 --> 00:12:32,205
[SPEAKER_00]: These are the five murderous enemies.

132
00:12:34,305 --> 00:12:36,006
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there you go now.

133
00:12:36,246 --> 00:12:39,288
[SPEAKER_01]: You can practice that.

134
00:12:40,649 --> 00:12:42,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing the danger in the four elements.

135
00:12:42,510 --> 00:12:49,255
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you develop this enchantment towards the form and anything that comes out of it.

136
00:12:53,918 --> 00:13:01,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you can then carry on and do the same towards perception, feeling, intentions.

137
00:13:02,565 --> 00:13:03,466
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, other aspects.

138
00:13:03,506 --> 00:13:07,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can easily overlook, okay, well, this is not physical, this is not material.

139
00:13:09,191 --> 00:13:15,456
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, certain significance of some feelings, ideas, notions.

140
00:13:15,516 --> 00:13:16,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind you, it's always physical.

141
00:13:16,577 --> 00:13:18,980
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't have those things if you don't have the form.

142
00:13:20,741 --> 00:13:24,985
[SPEAKER_01]: But nevertheless, you know, the mind can sort of find its ways of

143
00:13:27,624 --> 00:13:30,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Keeping its ignorance, feeding it.

144
00:13:30,746 --> 00:13:32,367
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to reflect further.

145
00:13:33,328 --> 00:13:40,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so, if you approach it through the five aggregates, you think you outran the four great elements, because you withdrew from the attachment to the body, maybe.

146
00:13:41,292 --> 00:13:47,716
[SPEAKER_01]: But, how about now attachment to your intentions, things you want to do, things you feel, things you perceive.

147
00:13:47,917 --> 00:13:49,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you enjoy perceiving?

148
00:13:49,778 --> 00:13:56,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it's effortless, even if you're not seeking out these perceptions, do you still enjoy the fact that you can perceive?

149
00:13:57,088 --> 00:13:58,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you still bound with that?

150
00:14:00,030 --> 00:14:08,156
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all these things that come as a result of those four great elements, parietal consciousness, the five aggregates, they're the murderous enemies.

151
00:14:09,297 --> 00:14:20,425
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you withdraw yourself from the from the four snakes, now you're on the run from the five hired assassins, hired murderers, they're seeking out to kill you on the spot.

152
00:14:22,083 --> 00:14:28,726
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you get to meet these five, how would you... what would your attitude be towards them?

153
00:14:29,067 --> 00:14:30,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Hey, how's it going?

154
00:14:30,567 --> 00:14:31,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's talk about this.

155
00:14:31,828 --> 00:14:32,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, there's no talk.

156
00:14:33,189 --> 00:14:34,229
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, they'll kill you on sight.

157
00:14:34,909 --> 00:14:37,030
[SPEAKER_01]: If they see you, they'll kill you.

158
00:14:37,131 --> 00:14:38,011
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you would hide.

159
00:14:38,971 --> 00:14:45,515
[SPEAKER_01]: You would not even want to be seen by form, by feeling, by perception, by intention, by consciousness.

160
00:14:46,075 --> 00:14:47,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Let alone engage with it.

161
00:14:48,256 --> 00:14:49,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Let alone try to reason with it.

162
00:14:51,063 --> 00:14:55,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Being seen means getting killed.

163
00:14:55,067 --> 00:15:04,556
[SPEAKER_01]: So, next time, when there is a lingering feeling or something that you're acting out of, can you act out of a feeling, if that feeling doesn't even see you?

164
00:15:05,277 --> 00:15:06,458
[SPEAKER_01]: If you make sure you do not

165
00:15:07,909 --> 00:15:09,349
[SPEAKER_01]: You present yourself to that feeling.

166
00:15:09,429 --> 00:15:10,250
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't take it up.

167
00:15:10,790 --> 00:15:11,450
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't engage.

168
00:15:11,470 --> 00:15:13,050
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't say, hey, hey, I'm here, I'm here.

169
00:15:13,110 --> 00:15:15,231
[SPEAKER_00]: You keep it at a distance.

170
00:15:15,251 --> 00:15:16,951
[SPEAKER_00]: You keep yourself at a distance.

171
00:15:17,151 --> 00:15:17,711
[SPEAKER_01]: From the feeling.

172
00:15:17,731 --> 00:15:18,772
[SPEAKER_01]: From the risen feeling.

173
00:15:18,792 --> 00:15:20,512
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't keep the feeling at a distance.

174
00:15:20,932 --> 00:15:26,154
[SPEAKER_01]: The murderous assassins, they go as they please and come and seek you.

175
00:15:26,594 --> 00:15:29,634
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't keep yourself at a distance, so they don't kill you.

176
00:15:29,774 --> 00:15:33,275
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the hiding place from the aggregates?

177
00:15:33,295 --> 00:15:34,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Is keeping yourself at a distance.

178
00:15:34,536 --> 00:15:35,116
[SPEAKER_00]: What does that do?

179
00:15:35,396 --> 00:15:35,936
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you do it?

180
00:15:36,536 --> 00:15:37,257
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you keep yourself?

181
00:15:37,277 --> 00:15:39,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, by not acting out of it, to begin with.

182
00:15:40,079 --> 00:15:41,800
[SPEAKER_01]: By not welcoming the assassin into your home.

183
00:15:42,640 --> 00:15:45,062
[SPEAKER_01]: By not seeking out and trying to fight back.

184
00:15:46,123 --> 00:15:49,805
[SPEAKER_01]: By not pretending that, oh, no, he's not... we'll talk about it, I'm fine.

185
00:15:49,905 --> 00:15:50,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasant feeling.

186
00:15:51,026 --> 00:15:52,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

187
00:15:52,367 --> 00:15:57,170
[SPEAKER_01]: That's usually how the... if somebody wants to kill you, that's usually how it will work.

188
00:15:57,530 --> 00:15:58,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Lure you out.

189
00:15:59,300 --> 00:16:02,101
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole... that's the whole premise of obeit.

190
00:16:04,701 --> 00:16:12,844
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you would make yourself unknown for the feeling, for the intention, for the consciousness, for the aggregates.

191
00:16:15,284 --> 00:16:25,207
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have your experience at home, whatever mood is enduring, whatever feeling is enduring, whatever, like, pressure towards action, towards sensuality, towards ill-will is enduring.

192
00:16:26,938 --> 00:16:28,520
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't try to get rid of that.

193
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:33,284
[SPEAKER_01]: That would be like trying to chase away the murderer, the five murderers.

194
00:16:34,566 --> 00:16:35,987
[SPEAKER_01]: So you don't try to get rid of it.

195
00:16:36,788 --> 00:16:37,909
[SPEAKER_01]: And you certainly don't...

196
00:16:37,929 --> 00:16:39,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I'll just do what they tell me to.

197
00:16:40,932 --> 00:16:42,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that results in your death.

198
00:16:42,774 --> 00:16:45,957
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you say, come out, come out of hiding.

199
00:16:45,977 --> 00:16:46,678
[SPEAKER_01]: We're not going to harm you.

200
00:16:46,698 --> 00:16:47,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Come out of hiding.

201
00:16:47,358 --> 00:16:48,239
[SPEAKER_01]: And you come out of hiding.

202
00:16:49,681 --> 00:16:50,742
[SPEAKER_01]: So you don't act out of it.

203
00:16:51,821 --> 00:16:54,581
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't try to get rid of it, because that would also give away your position.

204
00:16:55,542 --> 00:17:04,003
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to allow it to endure, and by allowing it to endure, and not acting out of it, you get to understand the nature of it.

205
00:17:05,183 --> 00:17:20,546
[SPEAKER_01]: And certainly, if you still had any notions of control over your feelings, intentions and aggregates, you will not, because now you realize any form of being known or recognized by these things means death, means liability to death, to suffering.

206
00:17:23,170 --> 00:17:24,711
[SPEAKER_00]: It's your whole experience.

207
00:17:25,691 --> 00:17:27,952
[SPEAKER_00]: You're surrounded by a danger.

208
00:17:30,333 --> 00:17:37,636
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, literally, like, not even... not even, like, you're surrounded...

209
00:17:37,656 --> 00:17:45,359
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, figuratively speaking, I understand, but the point is, don't think that you have, like, a sixth place where you can hide from these five

210
00:17:46,682 --> 00:17:47,182
[SPEAKER_01]: murderers.

211
00:17:47,262 --> 00:17:47,422
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

212
00:17:47,663 --> 00:17:49,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Five murderers are your five stepping stones.

213
00:17:50,164 --> 00:17:52,245
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're always on someone's murdering fields.

214
00:17:52,305 --> 00:17:52,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

215
00:17:52,525 --> 00:17:52,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

216
00:17:53,285 --> 00:17:54,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Through your whole experience.

217
00:17:55,226 --> 00:17:55,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

218
00:17:55,686 --> 00:17:56,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Through...

219
00:17:56,246 --> 00:18:06,911
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, the only way to hide is to basically, like, literally remove the ownership, let go of it, make yourself invisible towards whatever pressure is there enduring in you.

220
00:18:07,452 --> 00:18:09,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's really the problem.

221
00:18:09,313 --> 00:18:12,074
[SPEAKER_01]: The people keep acting out of being contacted, being pressured.

222
00:18:13,532 --> 00:18:17,376
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the root of all problems for every individual.

223
00:18:17,936 --> 00:18:22,219
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were not acting out, these things would not deceive you then even more.

224
00:18:23,320 --> 00:18:27,604
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever feeling you have towards, like, anger, frustration, you don't act out.

225
00:18:28,605 --> 00:18:29,806
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's always the problem.

226
00:18:29,826 --> 00:18:32,047
[SPEAKER_01]: When the problem does arise, it's after people acted out.

227
00:18:33,429 --> 00:18:38,633
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were not acting, if you were not committing karma on account of these things, these things would not see you.

228
00:18:39,741 --> 00:18:49,665
[SPEAKER_01]: They would just come, threaten, shout, try to pressure you towards action, or lure you out with sensuality, sort of enchant you with beautiful sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, all of that.

229
00:18:50,205 --> 00:18:58,708
[SPEAKER_01]: But still, if you were not choosing to step into that bait, domain of bait, domain of a trap, these things can opposites forever.

230
00:18:58,748 --> 00:18:59,348
[SPEAKER_01]: They would move on.

231
00:18:59,488 --> 00:19:05,450
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's usually an individual who cannot bear that pressure, bear that pull, bear that enchantment.

232
00:19:05,671 --> 00:19:06,731
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's the problem, really.

233
00:19:07,945 --> 00:19:10,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you can say, okay, so I'll just never act out of these things.

234
00:19:11,329 --> 00:19:15,995
[SPEAKER_01]: That's fine, but these things are so deeply rooted, it's simply saying no to it and enduring it.

235
00:19:16,796 --> 00:19:25,607
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to start like that, but then you really... you need wisdom as well, in order to be able to actually fulfill not acting out of it.

236
00:19:27,588 --> 00:19:31,491
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, wisdom... yeah, for that you need instruction, as we said in previous talks.

237
00:19:31,831 --> 00:19:44,922
[SPEAKER_01]: But at the same time, no amount of instruction, unless you're restrained first, unless your senses are restrained, as in you are restrained in regard to your senses, no amount of wisdom can take place, even if the Buddha himself is teaching you there, step by step.

238
00:19:45,402 --> 00:19:47,223
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you do need to be restrained.

239
00:19:47,264 --> 00:19:55,250
[SPEAKER_01]: You do need to at least, out of a principle, not act when you're pressured by pleasure, by pain, by neutral feeling.

240
00:19:59,529 --> 00:20:03,773
[SPEAKER_01]: So, see, you're already emulating acting... like, non-acting.

241
00:20:03,793 --> 00:20:07,057
[SPEAKER_01]: You're already emulating being invisible to the murderers.

242
00:20:08,698 --> 00:20:11,541
[SPEAKER_01]: And once you get to understand, then it becomes even easier.

243
00:20:11,801 --> 00:20:15,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you actually learn that there is this whole way of... you can move around without being seen.

244
00:20:15,965 --> 00:20:17,507
[SPEAKER_01]: And Buddha often says that Mara can't see you.

245
00:20:18,780 --> 00:20:23,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Develop the right view, practice full abundance in reality, become... Mara cannot... you're invisible.

246
00:20:23,241 --> 00:20:24,221
[SPEAKER_01]: Mara cannot see you.

247
00:20:24,301 --> 00:20:26,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, what he means by Mara is those five aggregates.

248
00:20:26,662 --> 00:20:27,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure.

249
00:20:27,222 --> 00:20:29,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Five aggregates, senses, whatever.

250
00:20:29,222 --> 00:20:29,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is Mara.

251
00:20:29,962 --> 00:20:31,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything that appears belongs to Mara.

252
00:20:32,483 --> 00:20:34,924
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're engaging with it, you're engaging with Mara's possessions.

253
00:20:35,004 --> 00:20:37,064
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he has every right to do whatever he wants with you.

254
00:20:40,085 --> 00:20:43,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you do it willingly.

255
00:20:43,325 --> 00:20:44,926
[SPEAKER_01]: Grab the bait, willingly.

256
00:20:44,946 --> 00:20:45,686
[SPEAKER_01]: You go after it.

257
00:20:58,024 --> 00:21:06,953
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, afraid of the four vipers, of fierce heat and deadly venom, and of the five murderous enemies, that man would flee in one direction or another.

258
00:21:08,015 --> 00:21:14,802
[SPEAKER_00]: They would tell him, good man, a sixth murderer, an intimate companion, is pursuing you with a drawn sword.

259
00:21:14,982 --> 00:21:18,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking, wherever I see him, I will cut off his head right on the spot.

260
00:21:18,846 --> 00:21:20,408
[SPEAKER_00]: Do whatever has to be done, good man.

261
00:21:24,305 --> 00:21:27,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Delight and lust.

262
00:21:27,267 --> 00:21:30,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

263
00:21:30,990 --> 00:21:32,892
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you see, it's getting subtler and subtler.

264
00:21:33,152 --> 00:21:33,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

265
00:21:34,973 --> 00:21:37,715
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can, okay, I see the peril of the four vipers.

266
00:21:38,316 --> 00:21:41,038
[SPEAKER_01]: I see the peril of these five murderers.

267
00:21:41,638 --> 00:21:49,945
[SPEAKER_01]: But still, it's even harder than, it's even subtler than to see the peril of your own delight.

268
00:21:51,967 --> 00:21:57,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Towards anything, pretty much, however subtle it might be in your experience.

269
00:21:58,832 --> 00:21:59,953
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what I was saying.

270
00:22:00,193 --> 00:22:14,865
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like when you... you choose to accept pleasure, you choose to accept pain and try to get rid of it, you choose to try to distract yourself with mutual feeling carelessly, and that delight that arises in regard to these feelings,

271
00:22:16,250 --> 00:22:18,431
[SPEAKER_01]: That's even a subtle thing you need to see as dangerous.

272
00:22:19,352 --> 00:22:25,455
[SPEAKER_01]: As in your own intention to give in is the most dangerous thing.

273
00:22:25,936 --> 00:22:28,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's always coated in that delight.

274
00:22:28,697 --> 00:22:29,838
[SPEAKER_01]: How does delight result in?

275
00:22:30,798 --> 00:22:32,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Always giving in.

276
00:22:33,160 --> 00:22:35,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Because delightful means friendly.

277
00:22:37,102 --> 00:22:38,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Delightful means agreeable.

278
00:22:39,628 --> 00:22:40,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-threatening.

279
00:22:40,469 --> 00:22:43,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Friendly means not dangerous, because it's friendly.

280
00:22:43,593 --> 00:22:45,515
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you... Well, again, imagine seeing a good friend.

281
00:22:46,977 --> 00:22:47,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Delightful.

282
00:22:48,899 --> 00:22:49,740
[SPEAKER_01]: A delightful person.

283
00:22:49,760 --> 00:22:51,963
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing to fear in regard to this person.

284
00:22:55,198 --> 00:22:57,519
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the way compared to intimate companion.

285
00:22:58,039 --> 00:23:11,544
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the closest friendly phenomenon you regard yourself, for yourself, your own delight, which is actually completely there on a... on a... on a false pretenses, as your friend.

286
00:23:11,584 --> 00:23:12,464
[SPEAKER_00]: He's just there to kill you.

287
00:23:14,865 --> 00:23:22,548
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that delight, that always results in you giving in to this or that, because you don't see the danger on account of delight,

288
00:23:23,987 --> 00:23:28,888
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to not do that, would you have to worry about the vipers of the five murderers, no?

289
00:23:29,529 --> 00:23:32,729
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the delight that always sends you back to them, carelessly.

290
00:23:36,851 --> 00:23:45,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were not delighting in anything, you'll never make yourself known or visible to the murderers, and you wouldn't have to look after the vipers.

291
00:23:46,683 --> 00:23:48,284
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the doorway.

292
00:23:48,444 --> 00:23:50,705
[SPEAKER_01]: That's pretty much that.

293
00:23:50,825 --> 00:23:54,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything around you can be bait set by Mara.

294
00:23:56,088 --> 00:23:59,070
[SPEAKER_01]: But none of that bait can pounce and bait you by itself.

295
00:24:00,190 --> 00:24:05,113
[SPEAKER_01]: You are always the one who makes that first and the last step towards it.

296
00:24:06,113 --> 00:24:12,977
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you were to learn to live surrounded by the bait and simply remove the slightest delight in engaging with the bait,

297
00:24:14,473 --> 00:24:14,794
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

298
00:24:14,914 --> 00:24:15,936
[SPEAKER_01]: You're free.

299
00:24:15,956 --> 00:24:17,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Hunter cannot see you.

300
00:24:17,739 --> 00:24:20,585
[SPEAKER_01]: He can only see you once you're trapped.

301
00:24:20,605 --> 00:24:21,466
[SPEAKER_01]: He comes and checks the bait.

302
00:24:21,506 --> 00:24:22,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, look, there it is.

303
00:24:23,590 --> 00:24:25,133
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you never catch the bait...

304
00:24:26,539 --> 00:24:27,359
[SPEAKER_01]: This bait is useless.

305
00:24:27,459 --> 00:24:29,419
[SPEAKER_00]: You learn how to live around the bait.

306
00:24:29,439 --> 00:24:32,440
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're trying to find where... where is that delight?

307
00:24:32,520 --> 00:24:33,900
[SPEAKER_00]: How can I be mindful of the light?

308
00:24:34,920 --> 00:24:45,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, see, that's all... if you want to be mindful of the light, first be mindful of the four vipers, then be mindful of the five... five murderers, then you can see what's left there is your delight.

309
00:24:46,522 --> 00:24:47,543
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just...

310
00:24:47,563 --> 00:24:49,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Intention towards... Well, exactly.

311
00:24:49,343 --> 00:24:55,804
[SPEAKER_01]: The intention that's rooted in seeing non-danger or in not seeing danger.

312
00:24:59,344 --> 00:25:10,730
[SPEAKER_01]: So, are there things like, okay, so you're sensory-strained, you're celibate, you're moderate in eating, all that's great, all that's building up, but now you sit alone in the solitude.

313
00:25:10,810 --> 00:25:12,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Are there things you delight in?

314
00:25:13,171 --> 00:25:18,073
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, oh, let me, let me go for a delightful walk.

315
00:25:18,153 --> 00:25:19,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it done with a delight?

316
00:25:20,374 --> 00:25:25,497
[SPEAKER_01]: The walk is not the problem, it is a purpose, but it's a problem if it's done out of delight, if you're delighting in it.

317
00:25:28,193 --> 00:25:29,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, let me go to sleep.

318
00:25:30,534 --> 00:25:35,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I sort of joyful on account of the prospect of sleeping?

319
00:25:38,637 --> 00:25:38,977
[SPEAKER_00]: You know.

320
00:25:39,257 --> 00:25:39,597
[SPEAKER_01]: You know.

321
00:25:39,757 --> 00:25:40,077
[SPEAKER_01]: You know.

322
00:25:40,337 --> 00:25:40,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

323
00:25:41,798 --> 00:25:44,999
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize, ah, so now that's that delight.

324
00:25:45,959 --> 00:25:50,161
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not like, I'll never sleep or I'll never walk again because everything is sensuality.

325
00:25:50,221 --> 00:25:50,341
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

326
00:25:51,121 --> 00:25:52,402
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your delight that's the problem.

327
00:25:54,583 --> 00:25:55,623
[SPEAKER_01]: Walking is not sensual.

328
00:25:57,580 --> 00:26:04,824
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you do it out of delight, yeah, you still keep yourself bound with the central domain and other things, even if you remain perfectly restrained for the rest of your life.

329
00:26:05,604 --> 00:26:13,348
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to uproot sensuality and that whole peril that comes with it, delight is that gateway that must be shut.

330
00:26:14,549 --> 00:26:15,830
[SPEAKER_00]: It sounds quite depressing.

331
00:26:17,470 --> 00:26:18,351
[SPEAKER_01]: It does, it does.

332
00:26:18,371 --> 00:26:21,252
[SPEAKER_00]: You're surrounded by vipers, enemies, people trying to kill you.

333
00:26:22,033 --> 00:26:26,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's only depressing because your mind still wants things to be otherwise.

334
00:26:27,330 --> 00:26:31,311
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, like, you wouldn't be depressed if I tell you 2 and 2 is 4.

335
00:26:32,331 --> 00:26:34,252
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just... yeah, that's just how it is.

336
00:26:34,292 --> 00:26:34,972
[SPEAKER_01]: 2 and 2 is 4.

337
00:26:37,112 --> 00:26:41,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Only if you strongly wish for 2 and 2 to be 6, you'll be depressed to find out that 2 and 2 is 4.

338
00:26:41,794 --> 00:26:47,135
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not depressed because 2 and 2 is 4, you're depressed because of your own misconceived expectations.

339
00:26:48,157 --> 00:26:55,359
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're depressed on account of the vipers and the factual murderers and stuff, well, yeah, it's because you're ignoring it, because you want things to be different.

340
00:26:55,699 --> 00:26:58,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that are not in your control, you want to be different.

341
00:26:59,380 --> 00:27:01,321
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you yourself make yourself depressed.

342
00:27:02,061 --> 00:27:03,462
[SPEAKER_00]: That delight is... Yeah.

343
00:27:03,682 --> 00:27:04,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, delight.

344
00:27:04,722 --> 00:27:05,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

345
00:27:05,182 --> 00:27:08,983
[SPEAKER_01]: You delight in these other ideas that are contradictory to reality of things.

346
00:27:10,064 --> 00:27:12,604
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you remove that, there is no little... Like Arahant.

347
00:27:13,365 --> 00:27:14,145
[SPEAKER_01]: He's joyless.

348
00:27:15,458 --> 00:27:16,199
[SPEAKER_01]: He has no delight.

349
00:27:17,039 --> 00:27:19,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, that's the greatest joy in the world.

350
00:27:19,061 --> 00:27:22,784
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you develop it, the safety, cross the shore, you cannot be harmed.

351
00:27:22,804 --> 00:27:26,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're surrounded by all these... Factually, again, it's not optional.

352
00:27:27,568 --> 00:27:31,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, if I don't think about this, if I don't read these suttas, these murderers will not come.

353
00:27:31,851 --> 00:27:32,091
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

354
00:27:32,111 --> 00:27:35,914
[SPEAKER_01]: We're telling you how... what you are, what your life is, what the aggregates are.

355
00:27:36,294 --> 00:27:40,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Describing the nature from a greater perspective than from somebody who developed it.

356
00:27:41,587 --> 00:27:48,079
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, you can ignore it and then literally be like the easiest prey.

357
00:27:50,790 --> 00:27:52,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you can try and do something about it.

358
00:27:52,171 --> 00:27:53,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is, that's always...

359
00:27:53,732 --> 00:27:54,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, good man.

360
00:27:54,692 --> 00:27:55,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Do what you think is fit.

361
00:27:55,913 --> 00:27:58,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's fit to try and avoid this.

362
00:27:58,854 --> 00:27:59,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, that's the thing.

363
00:27:59,735 --> 00:28:04,357
[SPEAKER_01]: If you admit it, you realize the only fit thing to do is to try and overcome this.

364
00:28:05,658 --> 00:28:09,260
[SPEAKER_01]: He keeps running, hiding, and he says, okay, now I have to hide further, and further, and further.

365
00:28:09,961 --> 00:28:12,502
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the intimate companion, that's what it is.

366
00:28:12,882 --> 00:28:13,923
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to recognize

367
00:28:14,912 --> 00:28:28,799
[SPEAKER_01]: The most intimate point of view that you have, that delight that comes with it, with your intentions, even things like, okay, there are things that are obviously harmful, but now even things that are not harmful, you know, against the precepts and stuff, are you delighting mentally in it?

368
00:28:29,159 --> 00:28:32,540
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that delight is the harm, is the danger.

369
00:28:33,401 --> 00:28:41,004
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's hard to see, because that has always been with you, that's always the closest to you, that's always in the heart of every intention that you act out of.

370
00:28:41,425 --> 00:28:41,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Delight.

371
00:28:42,868 --> 00:28:48,111
[SPEAKER_01]: My trustworthy friend, since the day I was born, I know this friend of delight.

372
00:28:49,652 --> 00:28:52,374
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, now you find out, oh yeah, he's there also to kill you.

373
00:28:52,734 --> 00:28:54,495
[SPEAKER_01]: That was his sole purpose all this time.

374
00:28:55,876 --> 00:28:56,696
[SPEAKER_00]: Just to cut your head off.

375
00:28:56,756 --> 00:28:57,917
[SPEAKER_00]: Stabbing in the back the whole time.

376
00:28:57,937 --> 00:28:58,677
[SPEAKER_01]: When you fall asleep.

377
00:29:05,797 --> 00:29:14,279
[SPEAKER_00]: Then Bhikkhu is afraid of the four vipers of fierce heat and deadly venom, the five murderous enemies and the sixth murderer, the intimate companion with drawn sword.

378
00:29:14,919 --> 00:29:18,100
[SPEAKER_00]: That man would flee in one direction or another.

379
00:29:18,820 --> 00:29:21,841
[SPEAKER_00]: He would see an empty village.

380
00:29:21,861 --> 00:29:24,681
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever house he enters is void, deserted and empty.

381
00:29:24,881 --> 00:29:27,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever pot he takes hold is void, hollow and empty.

382
00:29:28,450 --> 00:29:32,791
[SPEAKER_00]: They would tell him, good man, just now village attackers will raid this empty village.

383
00:29:33,791 --> 00:29:35,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Do whatever has to be done, good man.

384
00:29:38,992 --> 00:29:43,813
[SPEAKER_00]: And the empty village, this is the designation for the six internal sense bases.

385
00:29:44,833 --> 00:29:53,815
[SPEAKER_00]: If bhikkhus, a wise, competent, intelligent person examines them by way of the eye, and so on, they appear to be void, hollow and empty.

386
00:29:56,596 --> 00:29:56,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Here.

387
00:29:57,748 --> 00:30:00,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so if you... that's now even subtler.

388
00:30:01,069 --> 00:30:03,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, don't rest on that either.

389
00:30:04,149 --> 00:30:18,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that which is void, hollow, empty, unownable, that you have hidden away from all these previous murderers, vipers and intimate companions, well, that also is about to be raided and swept away in a stampede of robbers and maniacs.

390
00:30:19,012 --> 00:30:20,533
[SPEAKER_01]: So, better keep moving.

391
00:30:21,613 --> 00:30:22,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Keep moving.

392
00:30:26,661 --> 00:30:29,423
[SPEAKER_00]: So, from the delight, moving away from... Yeah, yeah.

393
00:30:29,463 --> 00:30:33,685
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you uprooted the delight, but you still rest upon the hollowness.

394
00:30:35,006 --> 00:30:38,688
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like what we spoke in a few talks ago about non-being.

395
00:30:39,308 --> 00:30:39,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

396
00:30:39,869 --> 00:30:51,996
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, not delight... it's not the delight that you have towards being, but still, you... the fact that you're resting, that you're not moving further away, that you're not seeing the danger even of that hollow, unownable things, that you have no delight in,

397
00:30:53,256 --> 00:31:00,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, to that extent, there is still that ārūpa-bhāva that we spoke about.

398
00:31:00,707 --> 00:31:02,029
[SPEAKER_01]: The non-material.

399
00:31:03,277 --> 00:31:07,319
[SPEAKER_01]: So to speak, being that you are, that you exist in that form.

400
00:31:07,339 --> 00:31:17,845
[SPEAKER_01]: So there's no delight there, which is why often even in the suttas, an anāgāmī could just not think... he would think he's an arahant, overestimate himself.

401
00:31:18,646 --> 00:31:22,488
[SPEAKER_01]: There are a number of cases in the suttas, because things are so subtle now.

402
00:31:22,588 --> 00:31:26,270
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no delight, no centrality, nothing really from the world can harm you.

403
00:31:27,711 --> 00:31:29,832
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're fully confident, you fully recognize that.

404
00:31:29,952 --> 00:31:30,773
[SPEAKER_01]: See it for yourself.

405
00:31:32,363 --> 00:31:39,365
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is the point that, yeah, okay, see now, those deep down inside, the hollowness, all that that you understood, abandon that as well.

406
00:31:41,986 --> 00:31:44,007
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that, too, will be swept away.

407
00:31:44,567 --> 00:31:48,289
[SPEAKER_00]: And to that extent, you will be exposed to suffering, in peril.

408
00:31:51,190 --> 00:31:56,451
[SPEAKER_01]: But by all means, everybody who is still dealing with the vipers, find an empty village.

409
00:31:56,511 --> 00:31:59,312
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be safe there, relatively speaking.

410
00:31:59,432 --> 00:32:00,753
[SPEAKER_01]: In comparison, you'll be safe.

411
00:32:01,620 --> 00:32:09,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Eventually, abandon that as well, but not before you have abandoned everything else that's actively seeking out to destroy you.

412
00:32:09,684 --> 00:32:09,964
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

413
00:32:09,984 --> 00:32:16,067
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know, the village attackers, this is a designation for the six external sense bases.

414
00:32:18,795 --> 00:32:23,077
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the eye is attacked by agreeable and disagreeable forms.

415
00:32:23,117 --> 00:32:23,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

416
00:32:23,858 --> 00:32:25,438
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still, as I said, it's still pressured.

417
00:32:25,939 --> 00:32:29,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're not acting out, you're not delighting, it's still being harassed by it.

418
00:32:30,021 --> 00:32:32,442
[SPEAKER_00]: And sooner or later, it will be destroyed by the world.

419
00:32:32,482 --> 00:32:34,503
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to that extent, you are... are you there?

420
00:32:34,623 --> 00:32:34,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

421
00:32:35,984 --> 00:32:38,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it doesn't matter if it's agreeable or disagreeable.

422
00:32:38,385 --> 00:32:41,927
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that there are sights for the eyes, that's disturbing in itself.

423
00:32:44,706 --> 00:32:47,367
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't then prevent... well, you already know that.

424
00:32:47,407 --> 00:32:48,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you wouldn't be in an empty village.

425
00:32:49,108 --> 00:32:56,691
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't prevent Saitam's Maltese touches from besetting and attacking and harassing the other... its internal counterparts.

426
00:32:57,591 --> 00:33:05,255
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can certainly then remove even the subtlest notion of resting upon those internal counterparts.

427
00:33:07,015 --> 00:33:10,897
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you will not be disturbed.

428
00:33:11,537 --> 00:33:12,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Not attacked by it, yeah.

429
00:33:15,627 --> 00:33:29,466
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, Bhikkhu's afraid of the four vipers of fierce heat, deadly venom, and five murderous enemies, and of the sixth murderer, the intimate companion with the drawn sword, and the village attackers, that man would flee in one direction or another.

430
00:33:30,741 --> 00:33:38,111
[SPEAKER_00]: You would see a great expanse of water, whose near shore was dangerous and fearful, and whose further shore was safe and free from danger.

431
00:33:39,313 --> 00:33:45,782
[SPEAKER_00]: But there would be no ferry boat or bridge for crossing over from the near shore to the far shore.

432
00:33:47,493 --> 00:33:58,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Then the man would think, there is this great expanse of water, whose near shore is dangerous and fearful, and whose very further shore is safe and free from danger, but there is no ferry boat or bridge for crossing over.

433
00:33:58,822 --> 00:34:07,729
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me collect grass, twigs, branches and foliage and bind them together into a raft, so that by means of that raft, making an effort with my hands and feet...

434
00:34:07,809 --> 00:34:08,910
[SPEAKER_01]: I sail into nibbāna.

435
00:34:09,270 --> 00:34:13,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I can get to safely cross that far shore.

436
00:34:13,353 --> 00:34:14,434
[SPEAKER_00]: And but this...

437
00:34:16,976 --> 00:34:20,637
[SPEAKER_00]: And being crossed over, gone beyond, the Brahman stands on high ground.

438
00:34:21,398 --> 00:34:33,203
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's always said for an arhant, that he's the only one that's thoroughly, fully reached the safety in this very life.

439
00:34:33,223 --> 00:34:39,986
[SPEAKER_00]: The great expanse of water is a designation for the four floods, sensuality, bhava,

440
00:34:41,386 --> 00:34:42,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Views, ignorance.

441
00:34:42,527 --> 00:34:43,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance, yes.

442
00:34:43,128 --> 00:34:43,989
[SPEAKER_01]: You must cross it all.

443
00:34:44,329 --> 00:34:47,452
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't cross two, or half, or third of it.

444
00:34:49,134 --> 00:34:50,375
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to cross the whole.

445
00:34:51,056 --> 00:34:57,142
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you can even be very close to the other shore, but you're still not on the other shore.

446
00:34:57,262 --> 00:34:58,023
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still not safe.

447
00:34:58,223 --> 00:34:59,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're like two meters away from the

448
00:35:00,866 --> 00:35:09,218
[SPEAKER_01]: safe shore, and all the way you left the bad shore, you're still... you're still not... you're still not safe.

449
00:35:10,199 --> 00:35:16,388
[SPEAKER_00]: And that near shore, which is dangerous and fearful, is a designation for identity.

450
00:35:20,113 --> 00:35:21,275
[SPEAKER_00]: It's assumption of mind.

451
00:35:21,976 --> 00:35:29,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Assumption of mind, ownership, I am, sense of self, just whichever way you take it, that's what it is.

452
00:35:29,489 --> 00:35:31,973
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where, that's what identifies you, that's what defines you.

453
00:35:32,454 --> 00:35:33,816
[SPEAKER_00]: With a near shore, right here.

454
00:35:35,403 --> 00:35:36,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what I mean.

455
00:35:36,423 --> 00:35:40,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, through that, you see, even with that delight, that's already kind of in the heart of your being.

456
00:35:40,984 --> 00:35:46,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Delight, that kind of energy for life that you have, pretty much, that's already affected with delight.

457
00:35:46,986 --> 00:35:49,606
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you abandon delight and not lose energy for life?

458
00:35:53,807 --> 00:35:55,308
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by seeing the danger of it.

459
00:35:56,248 --> 00:36:04,590
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the... that arahant in Therigata said, I delight not in life, I delight not in death.

460
00:36:05,905 --> 00:36:08,086
[SPEAKER_01]: I await my time like hireling his wage.

461
00:36:08,327 --> 00:36:13,390
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, I completed my work, I'm waiting for the reward, for the payment of parinibbāna.

462
00:36:14,530 --> 00:36:17,832
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he doesn't want to die, but he certainly doesn't want to live either.

463
00:36:18,973 --> 00:36:20,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Because life means engaging with the vipers.

464
00:36:21,014 --> 00:36:26,658
[SPEAKER_01]: Life means engaging with the murderous, sort of, assassins.

465
00:36:28,730 --> 00:36:33,215
[SPEAKER_01]: It means death, it means peril, it's agreeable, disagreeable, pressure, that whole thing.

466
00:36:33,535 --> 00:36:33,956
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a mess.

467
00:36:34,736 --> 00:36:35,838
[SPEAKER_01]: It's truly a peril.

468
00:36:36,118 --> 00:36:41,543
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize there is absolutely nowhere that you can rest upon.

469
00:36:43,105 --> 00:36:49,632
[SPEAKER_01]: No, absolutely no room to set your foot that you are safe here, unless you abandon it all.

470
00:36:52,963 --> 00:36:54,784
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's same for this life.

471
00:36:57,645 --> 00:37:03,248
[SPEAKER_01]: If you look at it from the point of view of saṃsāra, every single time, it's always like that.

472
00:37:05,689 --> 00:37:11,272
[SPEAKER_01]: So, constant, constant peril from every direction.

473
00:37:12,792 --> 00:37:14,073
[SPEAKER_01]: And you are the one ignoring it.

474
00:37:15,154 --> 00:37:19,058
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like these things are not there to be seen and understood.

475
00:37:19,078 --> 00:37:19,218
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

476
00:37:19,258 --> 00:37:22,301
[SPEAKER_00]: You are choosing to look away and pretend it's not there.

477
00:37:22,361 --> 00:37:35,373
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why beings, due to their own actions, keep themselves bound, like that reappearance constantly wandering around, roaming around, that shore of saṃsāra, where everything is just out to get you.

478
00:37:38,900 --> 00:37:41,102
[SPEAKER_01]: And all because you don't see the danger.

479
00:37:41,823 --> 00:37:43,624
[SPEAKER_01]: If you see the danger, you would not delight in it.

480
00:37:44,065 --> 00:37:45,566
[SPEAKER_01]: It would not be possible to delight in it.

481
00:37:51,471 --> 00:37:56,076
[SPEAKER_00]: With the further shore, which is safe and free from danger, this is the designation for nibbāna.

482
00:37:57,017 --> 00:37:59,219
[SPEAKER_00]: The raft is a noble eightfold path.

483
00:38:00,079 --> 00:38:03,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Making an effort with hands and feet, this is the arousing of energy.

484
00:38:05,692 --> 00:38:12,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but by the way, you could even just immediately skip the other bits and start making your own, as soon as you realize about the vipers.

485
00:38:12,839 --> 00:38:13,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's us.

486
00:38:13,800 --> 00:38:14,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you don't need to wait.

487
00:38:16,703 --> 00:38:25,952
[SPEAKER_01]: But the Buddha described the whole process, and as he was describing that the empty villagers, the man with wisdom, with discernment, would see that, which means without, most people would not even see.

488
00:38:26,433 --> 00:38:28,395
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't even know what internal sense bases are.

489
00:38:31,541 --> 00:38:34,263
[SPEAKER_01]: So, either of these things... Yeah, if you can, great.

490
00:38:34,283 --> 00:38:35,884
[SPEAKER_01]: If you already have wisdom to discern it, great.

491
00:38:36,545 --> 00:38:53,696
[SPEAKER_01]: But either of these things, that is the vipers, the five murderers, the intimate companion, it's enough for you to want to seek the other shore.

492
00:38:53,977 --> 00:38:57,179
[SPEAKER_01]: So, start making your raft, as soon as you realize that danger.

493
00:38:57,239 --> 00:38:58,160
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't spend your time

494
00:38:59,195 --> 00:39:00,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeking safety on that shore.

495
00:39:01,036 --> 00:39:08,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as I said, you know, five murderers... five murderers are relatively... relatively safer than the four vipers.

496
00:39:10,582 --> 00:39:15,664
[SPEAKER_01]: An intimate companion is relatively safer than five murderers and four vipers.

497
00:39:16,585 --> 00:39:17,125
[SPEAKER_01]: It's subtler.

498
00:39:18,946 --> 00:39:21,868
[SPEAKER_01]: An empty village is relatively safer than intimate companion.

499
00:39:23,580 --> 00:39:27,184
[SPEAKER_01]: But either way, you're still on that shore, and one way or the other, you will be killed by something.

500
00:39:28,285 --> 00:39:29,927
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you better abandon it.

501
00:39:30,808 --> 00:39:36,374
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you start abandoning it, then you'll be going through all these...

502
00:39:37,575 --> 00:39:41,480
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want to say stages, because it's a similes, they're not necessarily stages, but you will be...

503
00:39:42,422 --> 00:39:57,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if on account of vipers you want to practice the dhamma, build a raft and cross into safety, you will get to see the relative safety of the internal bases, the relative safety of just the delight.

504
00:39:57,068 --> 00:40:00,109
[SPEAKER_01]: If I control delight, if I don't give in to delight, I'm safe.

505
00:40:02,049 --> 00:40:07,171
[SPEAKER_01]: But all of that is still on that dangerous, perilous shore.

506
00:40:07,211 --> 00:40:08,052
[SPEAKER_01]: So the point is to

507
00:40:09,368 --> 00:40:10,889
[SPEAKER_01]: To actually seek all the way out.

508
00:40:11,309 --> 00:40:20,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Because often would happen even in the... even those who did understand the dhamma, they would stop perceiving the danger sufficiently enough, and then would not become arahants.

509
00:40:20,932 --> 00:40:22,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Not at least in this life.

510
00:40:23,113 --> 00:40:24,313
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what Guru would say.

511
00:40:24,333 --> 00:40:28,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if he meet the Sotāpanna, he would urge them, like basically steer the sense of urgency.

512
00:40:28,775 --> 00:40:29,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Scare them.

513
00:40:30,276 --> 00:40:31,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Scare them, literally.

514
00:40:31,896 --> 00:40:32,136
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

515
00:40:32,456 --> 00:40:33,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they still have work to do.

516
00:40:34,722 --> 00:40:35,963
[SPEAKER_01]: They still haven't abandoned the village.

517
00:40:36,343 --> 00:40:37,684
[SPEAKER_01]: They still haven't abandoned the delight.

518
00:40:38,104 --> 00:40:39,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, they abandoned the vipers.

519
00:40:39,184 --> 00:40:41,146
[SPEAKER_01]: They probably abandoned a great deal of fire murderers.

520
00:40:41,926 --> 00:40:43,227
[SPEAKER_01]: They understood the nibbāna.

521
00:40:43,247 --> 00:40:44,928
[SPEAKER_01]: They understood the escape to the other shore.

522
00:40:45,748 --> 00:40:47,029
[SPEAKER_01]: But they haven't abandoned it fully.

523
00:40:47,589 --> 00:40:48,430
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it would scare them.

524
00:40:48,870 --> 00:40:56,294
[SPEAKER_01]: If he would meet sakadāgāmī or anāgāmī, he would also cause a real sense of urgency in them.

525
00:40:56,334 --> 00:40:56,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

526
00:40:56,914 --> 00:40:58,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they still have work to do.

527
00:40:59,035 --> 00:41:00,376
[SPEAKER_01]: If he would meet an arahant, he wouldn't.

528
00:41:00,556 --> 00:41:00,776
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

529
00:41:00,816 --> 00:41:02,077
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he doesn't have work to do.

530
00:41:04,674 --> 00:41:18,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, crossed over, gone beyond, the Brahman stands on high ground.

531
00:41:19,159 --> 00:41:20,899
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the designation for the arahant.

532
00:41:23,680 --> 00:41:30,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so, as depressing as it might sound, it's actually good news, because the sooner you realize the danger,

533
00:41:31,327 --> 00:41:36,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the sooner you start uprooting your own delight, because of which you're exposed to the danger.

534
00:41:36,670 --> 00:41:39,011
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not exposed to the danger, because of the danger.

535
00:41:39,931 --> 00:41:43,513
[SPEAKER_01]: You're exposed to the danger, because you keep exposing yourself to the danger that's out there.

536
00:41:44,073 --> 00:41:46,074
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's dangerous to you, because you keep going there.

537
00:41:46,554 --> 00:41:47,295
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop going there.

538
00:41:47,615 --> 00:41:48,035
[SPEAKER_01]: It stays.

539
00:41:48,155 --> 00:41:49,376
[SPEAKER_01]: The thing is, the murderers are there.

540
00:41:49,716 --> 00:41:53,318
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, they're no danger to you, because they can't see you.

541
00:41:53,358 --> 00:41:54,838
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the joy that can come.

542
00:41:54,858 --> 00:41:55,759
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's the true joy.

543
00:41:55,779 --> 00:41:57,360
[SPEAKER_00]: That you are actually safe from this thing.

544
00:41:57,380 --> 00:41:57,960
[SPEAKER_01]: That's true.

545
00:41:58,200 --> 00:41:59,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, by the way, safety

546
00:42:00,701 --> 00:42:05,405
[SPEAKER_01]: The kind of underlying wish, underlying project.

547
00:42:06,305 --> 00:42:13,791
[SPEAKER_01]: Even when you do engage with these things, you do it because you want that outcome of satisfaction, peace, non-disturbance, safety.

548
00:42:14,111 --> 00:42:21,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Even when you engage with sensuality, even when you try to angrily get rid of something, it's always because you want that outcome of, I don't want to be bothered.

549
00:42:22,540 --> 00:42:33,588
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you truly recognize that as the universal goal of every individual to not be bothered, to be at ease, which it is the goal, then you realize, okay, so now I have a choice how I go about it.

550
00:42:34,989 --> 00:42:50,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I go by acting out of these things and hoping ease will come or do I actually start thinking about it on a deeper level and see actually is there something else I can do that is different to these obviously insufficient attempts, because, you know,

551
00:42:52,425 --> 00:43:02,931
[SPEAKER_01]: The best-case scenario in the life of centrality, non-restraint, is a very, very fragile and relative peace you get from the life.

552
00:43:03,011 --> 00:43:09,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Even when there's nothing, you know, no great misfortune, everything is fine, you're still never at ease completely.

553
00:43:09,354 --> 00:43:09,875
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't be.

554
00:43:11,796 --> 00:43:12,236
[SPEAKER_01]: But you want to.

555
00:43:12,756 --> 00:43:15,198
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to be at ease.

556
00:43:15,598 --> 00:43:16,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody wants to be at ease.

557
00:43:16,959 --> 00:43:18,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody will say no to ease.

558
00:43:20,137 --> 00:43:23,839
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to bathe the thing, feed the thing, exercise.

559
00:43:23,879 --> 00:43:25,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Things will get sick, things will get grumpy.

560
00:43:26,801 --> 00:43:27,801
[SPEAKER_01]: All you did everything right.

561
00:43:28,702 --> 00:43:30,383
[SPEAKER_01]: The vipers just get cranky sometimes.

562
00:43:32,504 --> 00:43:33,384
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just a slave.

563
00:43:33,524 --> 00:43:34,805
[SPEAKER_01]: You see, they quickly change.

564
00:43:34,845 --> 00:43:38,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes they're completely ignoring you, docile, and then just snap.

565
00:43:39,968 --> 00:43:46,471
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what the body is, what the health is, what the life is, what the youth is, what the strength is.

566
00:43:46,511 --> 00:43:49,333
[SPEAKER_01]: It can just be swept away, just changes, probably.

567
00:43:54,998 --> 00:43:58,459
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it starts with those divine messengers.

568
00:43:59,539 --> 00:44:00,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Sickness, aging, death.

569
00:44:02,040 --> 00:44:10,563
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, divine messenger means you recognize that message as a future inevitability.

570
00:44:11,083 --> 00:44:15,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like you wait for it to become inevitable reality to deal with it.

571
00:44:15,965 --> 00:44:20,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you wait to be sick, aging and dying in order to get rid of it, it's too late.

572
00:44:22,490 --> 00:44:31,137
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to see that right now, within this life, that currently nothing bothering me, everything is fine, that's exactly fine, there's no problem with that.

573
00:44:31,618 --> 00:44:35,581
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, within that, what is this life, that is fine, liable to?

574
00:44:38,223 --> 00:44:39,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I have a say in it?

575
00:44:39,204 --> 00:44:39,424
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

576
00:44:39,544 --> 00:44:40,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I have a control in it?

577
00:44:40,905 --> 00:44:45,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, how much say do you have in controlling the viper's mood and behavior and action?

578
00:44:46,250 --> 00:44:47,251
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how much say you have.

579
00:44:48,331 --> 00:44:48,532
[SPEAKER_00]: None.

580
00:44:49,632 --> 00:44:54,416
[SPEAKER_00]: A divine message is like a divine highlighter of the nature of what's going on.

581
00:44:54,436 --> 00:44:55,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Divine reminder.

582
00:44:55,596 --> 00:44:57,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Look, this is yours.

583
00:44:59,119 --> 00:45:02,862
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that it's not right now happening doesn't mean that you can avoid it.

584
00:45:07,165 --> 00:45:07,725
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do you do?

585
00:45:07,745 --> 00:45:09,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, don't stop avoiding it.

586
00:45:10,247 --> 00:45:12,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Keep it at the background of your mind all the time.

587
00:45:12,589 --> 00:45:13,990
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the mindfulness of death.

588
00:45:15,103 --> 00:45:19,607
[SPEAKER_01]: That the Buddha praised as the quickest way, but a bit dangerous as well, if you're not ready.

589
00:45:20,668 --> 00:45:21,048
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah.

590
00:45:21,629 --> 00:45:29,055
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever you do, good, joyful, whatever, just keep the context that you might be dead within the next in-and-out breath.

591
00:45:30,416 --> 00:45:35,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you will be unable to enjoy that delightful sensual sense.

592
00:45:36,641 --> 00:45:37,422
[SPEAKER_01]: You still do things.

593
00:45:37,942 --> 00:45:38,423
[SPEAKER_01]: You still eat.

594
00:45:39,604 --> 00:45:43,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Think about, like, you know, you have this person, people who are on death row.

595
00:45:44,911 --> 00:45:49,314
[SPEAKER_01]: About to be executed in the States, and then they were given the last meal.

596
00:45:49,835 --> 00:45:50,055
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

597
00:45:50,235 --> 00:45:50,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you want.

598
00:45:51,035 --> 00:45:52,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, whatever you want.

599
00:45:53,117 --> 00:45:55,919
[SPEAKER_01]: And it would come with this, like, insane sort of menu.

600
00:45:57,480 --> 00:45:58,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you enjoy it?

601
00:45:59,221 --> 00:45:59,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

602
00:46:00,482 --> 00:46:01,643
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter what they bring you.

603
00:46:01,983 --> 00:46:03,124
[SPEAKER_01]: The nicest, the tastiest food.

604
00:46:03,144 --> 00:46:05,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, you're about to get killed.

605
00:46:06,494 --> 00:46:08,395
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that cannot not be on your mind.

606
00:46:09,255 --> 00:46:15,437
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how would you relate to that meal when you cannot shake off the background of impending death?

607
00:46:16,578 --> 00:46:25,941
[SPEAKER_01]: That's mindfulness of death, if you would... but if you cultivate it, not if you're just subjected to it against your will and you're still trying to, like, resist in it internally.

608
00:46:26,041 --> 00:46:30,943
[SPEAKER_00]: If you actually practice like that, yeah, you would basically fade away all the delight in no time.

609
00:46:32,023 --> 00:46:33,564
[SPEAKER_00]: Or go mad in the process of doing so.

610
00:46:34,472 --> 00:46:37,213
[SPEAKER_01]: As many monks did in the suttas, the ones that were not ready.

611
00:46:38,773 --> 00:46:48,055
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha is like, okay, well, if you're not ready, maybe, you know, tone it down a bit, but don't feel excused on account of... Well, the Buddha said it's fine to tone it down.

612
00:46:48,535 --> 00:46:48,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

613
00:46:49,535 --> 00:46:53,076
[SPEAKER_01]: He said it's fine to take a different approach, but you're still at a perilous shore.

614
00:46:54,136 --> 00:46:57,917
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the outcome of that mindfulness of death still needs to be developed one way or the other.

615
00:46:58,877 --> 00:47:03,198
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's actually, you should be even with more sense of urgency, because mindfulness is the quickest way.

616
00:47:04,946 --> 00:47:09,050
[SPEAKER_01]: But now you... Now you're choosing the non-quickest way, because you can't handle the quickest way.

617
00:47:09,770 --> 00:47:16,176
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you should be twice as careful, because you will have... you're spending more time in a dangerous shore.

618
00:47:20,120 --> 00:47:24,584
[SPEAKER_00]: So, having that context of death... Yeah, danger.

619
00:47:24,704 --> 00:47:28,908
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't engage in sensuality to... To the extent of that delight, you can't.

620
00:47:30,479 --> 00:47:44,088
[SPEAKER_01]: And even that, by the way, even if you were to engage with sensuality, to give in to the intention, okay, I'll do this quickly or something, even to that momentary extent, you need to willingly choose to ignore the context of death.

621
00:47:44,569 --> 00:47:51,373
[SPEAKER_01]: If you willingly choose to not ignore the context of death, you will not be able to engage with sensuality, even if you decided to engage with sensuality.

622
00:47:51,853 --> 00:47:55,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Say you decide to engage with sensuality, I will eat this, the most sensual dinner,

623
00:48:00,496 --> 00:48:15,123
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you choose to engage with it, the only way you can do that is to ignore the fact that you'll be brutally murdered right after.

624
00:48:15,143 --> 00:48:19,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you don't ignore it, none of that will be... have any appeal.

625
00:48:21,026 --> 00:48:22,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally.

626
00:48:22,246 --> 00:48:22,906
[SPEAKER_01]: It's tasteless.

627
00:48:23,187 --> 00:48:23,887
[SPEAKER_01]: Tasteless, yeah.

628
00:48:24,867 --> 00:48:25,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Joyless.

629
00:48:26,753 --> 00:48:27,333
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's what I mean.

630
00:48:27,834 --> 00:48:29,255
[SPEAKER_01]: That joylessness, that's frightening.

631
00:48:29,275 --> 00:48:37,801
[SPEAKER_01]: And, like, when people did kind of get a glimpse of what the Buddha was teaching, they were like, oh, that... well, that will kill me, that will destroy me, that is just like utter... that's the worst.

632
00:48:38,021 --> 00:48:40,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Nibbāna you teach, and they would fall down on the ground in despair.

633
00:48:41,664 --> 00:48:43,405
[SPEAKER_01]: And, oh, man, that's... that's horrible.

634
00:48:44,386 --> 00:48:48,189
[SPEAKER_01]: But the... it's horrible not because it's horrible in itself.

635
00:48:48,429 --> 00:48:51,531
[SPEAKER_01]: It's horrible because of your expectations of that gratuitous joy and delight.

636
00:48:52,872 --> 00:48:54,434
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't depend on it,

637
00:48:55,393 --> 00:48:56,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Joylessness is great.

638
00:48:56,594 --> 00:48:58,636
[SPEAKER_01]: It's non-disturbing.

639
00:48:58,656 --> 00:48:59,256
[SPEAKER_01]: It's easy.

640
00:49:00,817 --> 00:49:01,718
[SPEAKER_00]: You're invisible to it.

641
00:49:01,998 --> 00:49:02,699
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not harmful.

642
00:49:02,739 --> 00:49:03,439
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not dangerous.

643
00:49:04,240 --> 00:49:05,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, there's passion.

644
00:49:05,601 --> 00:49:08,863
[SPEAKER_01]: But from the point of view of passion, this passion is the worst possible thing.

645
00:49:09,884 --> 00:49:13,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Like an addict trying to give up heroin.

646
00:49:14,788 --> 00:49:15,228
[SPEAKER_01]: The addiction.

647
00:49:18,711 --> 00:49:19,752
[SPEAKER_01]: All the reason won't help him.

648
00:49:20,232 --> 00:49:23,595
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still the most horrible thing you can imagine, being without this.

649
00:49:28,150 --> 00:49:30,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, what will the life be without this?

650
00:49:31,451 --> 00:49:34,312
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... it's just... it's too... it's too depressing to think about.

651
00:49:35,112 --> 00:49:37,653
[SPEAKER_01]: But not because the life will, in itself, be bad.

652
00:49:37,953 --> 00:49:45,035
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just because of your virtuously built-up, amplified assumptions and expectations, that you can equally undo.

653
00:49:46,736 --> 00:49:48,696
[SPEAKER_01]: All other people, loved ones, die the same.

654
00:49:49,677 --> 00:49:56,659
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to now readjust to the world without that which was always there, taken for granted, as yours, for you, and so on.

655
00:49:59,397 --> 00:50:04,000
[SPEAKER_01]: But the only reason you need readjustment is because you yourself drifted away from the reality of things.

656
00:50:05,941 --> 00:50:08,063
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the sooner you accept that, the sooner you readjust.

657
00:50:09,383 --> 00:50:11,885
[SPEAKER_00]: And sensuality, is that drifting away from reality?

658
00:50:12,125 --> 00:50:12,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Every time.

659
00:50:12,826 --> 00:50:13,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Every time.

660
00:50:13,746 --> 00:50:15,908
[SPEAKER_01]: It's perversion of reality, as the Buddha would say.

661
00:50:15,988 --> 00:50:16,748
[SPEAKER_00]: So, both?

662
00:50:16,968 --> 00:50:19,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Putting second as first, and first as second.

663
00:50:20,170 --> 00:50:21,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Distorting.

664
00:50:22,152 --> 00:50:25,234
[SPEAKER_01]: You're already aligning yourself with the right order of things.

665
00:50:28,965 --> 00:50:36,531
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're already revealing the nature of that sensuality, that it's pressuring and dragging you around, and you're an addict already.

666
00:50:36,891 --> 00:50:38,572
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're being mindful of death.

667
00:50:39,173 --> 00:50:40,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindful of death, yeah.

668
00:50:40,214 --> 00:50:41,374
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the result of...

669
00:50:41,675 --> 00:50:46,598
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, when I say mindful of death, it doesn't mean, like, whatever thought pops up in your mind, you go, I must do, I must think that, think that.

670
00:50:46,638 --> 00:50:46,959
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

671
00:50:48,440 --> 00:50:52,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Background context of death means you don't have to think about it, just don't forget about it.

672
00:50:56,612 --> 00:50:57,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm sitting here.

673
00:50:57,533 --> 00:50:58,013
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm drinking.

674
00:50:58,033 --> 00:50:58,594
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm getting up.

675
00:50:58,674 --> 00:50:59,875
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to have some water, maybe.

676
00:51:00,636 --> 00:51:01,937
[SPEAKER_01]: And I might die by the end of this day.

677
00:51:02,117 --> 00:51:03,258
[SPEAKER_01]: I might die by the end of the meal.

678
00:51:03,338 --> 00:51:04,520
[SPEAKER_01]: I might die by the end of this breath.

679
00:51:06,662 --> 00:51:07,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, that's a beautiful mountain.

680
00:51:07,883 --> 00:51:08,824
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a beautiful river.

681
00:51:09,524 --> 00:51:11,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and I might die by the end of this breath.

682
00:51:13,708 --> 00:51:15,370
[SPEAKER_01]: So, maintaining the context means

683
00:51:18,145 --> 00:51:24,635
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're able to maintain the context, means you're beginning to contrast everything against that ultimate, inevitable context.

684
00:51:25,536 --> 00:51:28,320
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you lose this enchantment with these things.

685
00:51:28,340 --> 00:51:31,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're enchanted with them, because you don't have the right contrast.

686
00:51:31,470 --> 00:51:41,798
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a common idea that if I keep the context of death in mind, that I will enjoy life more.

687
00:51:42,198 --> 00:51:42,378
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

688
00:51:42,399 --> 00:51:43,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, remember, you will die.

689
00:51:43,940 --> 00:51:44,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

690
00:51:44,320 --> 00:51:47,342
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that will be the result of that context of death.

691
00:51:47,362 --> 00:51:48,243
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not death.

692
00:51:48,623 --> 00:51:49,544
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not contemplation.

693
00:51:49,944 --> 00:51:53,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you don't really see that death pertains... because death pertains to right now.

694
00:51:54,811 --> 00:52:00,153
[SPEAKER_01]: When those people think that, they think of death in 20-30 years' time, which doesn't pertain to death.

695
00:52:00,773 --> 00:52:02,133
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an imaginary idea of death.

696
00:52:03,614 --> 00:52:05,954
[SPEAKER_01]: No, he doesn't know, but I might die literally in a second.

697
00:52:06,654 --> 00:52:10,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, then you feel it, and then you will not, oh, let me go and quickly do many things.

698
00:52:11,276 --> 00:52:16,497
[SPEAKER_01]: No, but I might literally die even before I start doing these things.

699
00:52:16,537 --> 00:52:18,598
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you want to get it real close.

700
00:52:19,330 --> 00:52:21,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you really want to make it pertain to you.

701
00:52:22,173 --> 00:52:32,802
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said, you should only do it if you have been keeping the precepts, been well restrained, not pursuing sensuality, because if you jump into it, it will crush you like a little bug.

702
00:52:33,944 --> 00:52:36,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Just anxiety will be too overwhelming.

703
00:52:36,465 --> 00:52:39,567
[SPEAKER_01]: It happened to the monks in the past, happened to the contemporary times.

704
00:52:40,147 --> 00:52:41,408
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, I met a number of them.

705
00:52:41,608 --> 00:52:43,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Many of them, like, lost their minds.

706
00:52:45,270 --> 00:52:47,891
[SPEAKER_01]: And this rope there was the best-case scenario.

707
00:52:49,032 --> 00:52:54,795
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, like, because it's playing with fire.

708
00:52:54,835 --> 00:52:58,737
[SPEAKER_01]: It can quickly burn all the chalices or completely incinerate you in the process of doing so.

709
00:52:59,965 --> 00:53:07,888
[SPEAKER_01]: And the whole sutta was when all those monks went and jumped off the cliff, and got that man to kill them, because they were so disgusted with it, and the death and death.

710
00:53:08,428 --> 00:53:09,569
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the Buddha is like, what happened?

711
00:53:09,589 --> 00:53:11,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you know that contemplation you gave us?

712
00:53:11,789 --> 00:53:12,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Killed half of the monks.

713
00:53:13,110 --> 00:53:13,890
[SPEAKER_01]: They couldn't handle it.

714
00:53:14,690 --> 00:53:17,952
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, okay, well then, do mindfulness of breathing instead, if you can't handle it.

715
00:53:18,772 --> 00:53:19,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Which still results in the same.

716
00:53:19,832 --> 00:53:21,213
[SPEAKER_01]: We spoke about that in other talks.

